      "id": 1,
      "text": "Founded in 1805 by New Englanders, Granville Ohio was named after Granville, Massachusetts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.granvilleoh.com/about-granville-ohio/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Granville sits atop a hill overlooking Denison University, which began in 1831 as Granville Theological and Literary Seminary.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granville%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village protects the Alligator Effigy Mound, a Fort Ancient structure built between 800 and 1200 CE and listed on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granville%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Buxton Inn, constructed in 1812, stands as one of Granville’s oldest surviving commercial buildings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granville%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the Underground Railroad era, Granville offered an alternative escape route when paths through Columbus were too dangerous.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
